# Copy this to `.env` and fill in what you need.
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` itself is gitignored and must stay that way — it is the only place a secret
# lives in this repository.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# READ THIS BEFORE YOU TRY TO BUY ANYTHING
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Please fill in the RAZORPAY pair below before running the purchase flow.
#
# Without it, NO ORDER CAN BE CREATED AT ALL. That surprises people, so here is the
# reason: an order is created UNPAID and no money moves at that step, but the merchant
# still has to build a payment instrument while creating it. Both instruments need the
# keys, and the Layer refuses `cod` for agent orders on purpose, so there is no third
# option to fall back on:
#
#     instrument "auto"  ->  500  this merchant has no Razorpay credentials configured
#     instrument "link"  ->  500  the same
#     instrument "cod"   ->  refused by the Layer, deliberately
#
# What DOES run with these left empty: all three shops, the catalog, live product
# detail, search across merchants, carts, the quantity and amount ceilings, the
# prompt-injection sanitizer and the audit log. Measured, per merchant:
#
#     conformance suite, per merchant:   55 passed, 3 skipped   (of 58)
#
# The three that skip say why, by name: one is structural (all three merchants offer
# all three payment modes, so "an undeclared mode" has no case left) and two are the
# payment-instrument tests, which name the missing variables rather than passing
# quietly. So the specification is fully checkable without an account. The product
# is not.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MERCHANT KEYS — required, and they are not secrets in any real sense
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each merchant authenticates exactly one caller: the Layer (SPEC §2.5). These are
# shared secrets between your Layer and your own merchants, so for a local checkout
# any string works. They must simply match what the merchant is started with.
NORTHWIND_AGENT_KEY=nw_agentkey_local_dev_only
VOLTLINE_AGENT_KEY=vl_agentkey_local_dev_only
MARIGOLD_AGENT_KEY=mg_agentkey_local_dev_only

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RAZORPAY — REQUIRED for any order, and free to obtain
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# TEST MODE ONLY, and it takes about two minutes:
#
#   1. sign in at https://dashboard.razorpay.com
#   2. switch the dashboard to TEST MODE (the toggle at the top) — please check this,
#      live keys will not work here and should never be pasted into a demo
#   3. Settings → API Keys → Generate Test Key
#   4. paste both values below, then restart the shops (each reads its keys at startup)
#
# The key id always begins with `rzp_test_`. If yours does not, you are on live mode.
# Nothing here ever charges a real card, and every order the demo creates is cancelled
# and refunded in the same run.
# The key id starts with `rzp_test_`. The secret never appears in any API response —
# merchants return only the publishable key id, and only for `checkout` orders.
#
# Note from experience, in case you try to pay a link by hand: the international test
# card 4111 1111 1111 1111 does NOT work on a fresh test account (international
# payments are off by default). Netbanking works, and so does UPI `success@razorpay`.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPENROUTER — optional; only for `demo/buyer.py`
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Drives a real LLM through the MCP tool surface, so the prompt-injection defence is
# measured against an actual model rather than asserted. Nothing else needs it. A
# whole purchase costs about $0.002 on gpt-4o-mini.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HOSTING — optional
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every merchant binds 127.0.0.1 on its own fixed port by default, which is what the
# scripts and the documentation assume. A host that hands you a port sets these.
# HOST=0.0.0.0
# PORT=8001
